{
  "item": "57",
  "target": "breakdown",
  "verdict": "WEAKENED",
  "attacks": [
    {
      "attack": "Paging without a defined ordering or cursor",
      "method": "Checked S1 AC1 and AC2 for page size, sort key, and cursor stability. AC2 requires 'no duplicates from the previous page' while new messages may be dead-lettered and redriven mid-browse.",
      "result": "No page size, ordering key, or cursor contract is stated. Offset-based paging over a changing DLQ can duplicate or skip messages, so AC2 cannot be tested as written."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Redrive-all snapshot semantics",
      "method": "Checked S4 AC1 ('every dead-lettered message on that subscription') against concurrent dead-lettering while the request runs.",
      "result": "The breakdown does not say whether the set is fixed at request time or grows during the operation. Messages dead-lettered after acceptance may or may not be included, and the AC cannot be verified either way."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Idempotency without a defined key or window",
      "method": "Checked S5 AC1 ('identical redrive request is submitted again'). Considered a message that is redriven, fails again, and is dead-lettered again before the duplicate request arrives.",
      "result": "Idempotency is keyed on an undefined 'identical request'. Nothing says whether the key is the request payload or the message id, or how long it is remembered. The second dead-letter is a new failure, so a literal reading of AC1 ('not re-enqueued') would suppress a legitimate redrive."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Redrive of an in-flight message",
      "method": "Read S5 AC2 against S3 AC1 and S4 AC1. Asked what happens when a selected id, or a message matched by redrive-all, is currently in-flight.",
      "result": "S5 AC2 says in-flight delivery is 'not duplicated or cancelled', but no AC says what happens to the redrive selection itself: rejected, skipped, or enqueued alongside the in-flight attempt. The two stories are not reconciled."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Partial-failure semantics",
      "method": "Compared S3 AC2 ('the request is rejected') with S3 AC1 ('exactly the selected messages are re-enqueued').",
      "result": "It is not stated whether a batch with one invalid id is fully rejected (all-or-nothing) or partially applied. S3 AC2 implies all-or-nothing, but no story says so, and no AC covers an id that was already redriven."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Subscription scoping of message ids",
      "method": "Checked S2 AC1 and AC2 for whether 'a dead-lettered message with a known id' is scoped to a subscription.",
      "result": "S2 does not say the lookup is scoped to a subscription. A message id from another subscription could be returned, and S2 AC2 does not cover the cross-subscription case."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Lifecycle of a redriven message",
      "method": "Asked what happens to the dead-letter record and the attempt counter after S3 or S4 re-enqueues a message.",
      "result": "No story says whether the record is removed, retained, or marked, or whether the attempt counter resets. Without this, a redrive can loop endlessly and the DLQ view can show stale or duplicate entries."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Terminology and scope drift from the body",
      "method": "Compared the body ('replay should be idempotent') with the stories (all say 'redrive').",
      "result": "The body uses replay and redrive interchangeably. The breakdown uses only redrive, so it is unclear whether a separate replay operation was intended. S4 is also a special case of S3 (select all) and could be merged."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Missing non-functional and security requirements",
      "method": "Checked all stories for operator authentication and authorization, payload redaction, page-size limits, batch-size limits, and audit logging.",
      "result": "None of these are covered. Full payloads are exposed to operators with no stated access control, and redrive has no batch limit or audit trail. The triage marks this as backend-only with no architecture change, which does not account for the delivery-path write."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "Paging contract is undefined, so the no-duplicates guarantee in S1 AC2 is untestable.",
      "evidence": "S1 AC1 says 'a bounded page' with no size. S1 AC2 says 'paging information from the prior response' with no cursor definition or ordering key."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Redrive-all has no snapshot boundary.",
      "evidence": "S4 AC1: 'every dead-lettered message on that subscription is re-enqueued'. No time or set boundary is given for concurrent dead-lettering."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Idempotency is underspecified and can suppress valid redrives.",
      "evidence": "S5 AC1: 'the identical redrive request is submitted again' has no key definition or time window. A message that fails again after redrive would be treated as a duplicate under a literal reading."
    },
    {
      "finding": "The in-flight rule in S5 AC2 is not connected to the redrive selection rule.",
      "evidence": "S5 AC2 covers in-flight delivery continuing unaffected, but S3 AC1 and S4 AC1 do not say whether in-flight ids are skipped, rejected, or re-enqueued."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Batch failure behaviour is ambiguous.",
      "evidence": "S3 AC2 says 'the request is rejected without re-enqueuing any message from it', which implies all-or-nothing, but S3 AC1 does not state this for the valid ids in the same request."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Message lookup is not scoped to a subscription.",
      "evidence": "S2 AC1 and AC2 refer to 'a dead-lettered message with a known id' with no subscription parameter, while every other story is subscription-scoped."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Redriven-message lifecycle is not specified.",
      "evidence": "No AC in S3 or S4 states what happens to the dead-letter record or the attempt counter after re-enqueue."
    },
    {
      "finding": "The breakdown ignores security, limits, and audit requirements that the body implies for operator tooling.",
      "evidence": "The body asks for operators to 'inspect a message's payload' and 'redrive ... all of them'. No story covers authorization, payload redaction, batch limits, or audit. The triage sets architecture_change: false and domains: [backend] despite a new write path onto the subscription."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Replay and redrive are conflated, and S4 duplicates S3.",
      "evidence": "The body says 'Replay should be idempotent'. S3 and S4 are both titled redrive, and S4 is S3 with all ids selected."
    }
  ]
}
```

