      "method": "Read the body sentence by sentence and map each requirement to a story and AC. Requirements checked: one-step creation, product identity fields, barcode uniqueness, zero-quantity rows valid, out-of-stock rendering, running out does not erase the product, and detail page display.",
      "result": "Most requirements are covered. The zero-quantity requirement is not: no story or AC relaxes the quantity > 0 rule or renders an 'out of stock' state. The 'running out must not erase it' requirement is also untraced. The core behavioral change the work item calls out has no acceptance test."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Trace the 'running out' path through consumption",
      "method": "Ask what happens when a pantry item's quantity is decremented to zero, since the work item says running out must not erase the product.",
      "result": "No story covers the decrement or consumption path. If that path currently deletes or rejects zero-quantity rows, the requirement fails even after S1-S4 land. I could not confirm current behavior without the code."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Submit blank optional fields through a real HTML form",
      "method": "Leave brand and barcode blank on the ingredient form and submit it. Then submit a second ingredient with a blank barcode, under the unique-when-present rule.",
      "result": "Browsers post empty strings, not null. Two blank barcodes stored as '' collide on the unique constraint, so S1 AC3 and S2 AC2 can fail in a literal implementation. The breakdown never requires blank-to-null normalization."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Edit an ingredient and resubmit its own barcode unchanged",
      "method": "Edit an existing ingredient that already has a barcode and save with that barcode untouched. Then edit another ingredient to take a barcode that is already in use.",
      "result": "No AC asserts that the self-resubmit succeeds. S2 AC3 only tests rejection on create (\"a barcode already used by another ingredient\") and does not clearly cover edit. A naive uniqueness check would reject a user's own barcode on save."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Force a partial failure inside the one-step Server Action",
      "method": "Make the ingredient insert succeed and the pantry-row insert fail, for example through a constraint or validation error on quantity.",
      "result": "S3 AC4 covers only the duplicate-barcode failure. It does not require the two inserts to be transactional, so an orphan ingredient without a pantry row can be left behind."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Submit partial or invalid package fields",
      "method": "Set packageQuantity without packageUnit, set packageUnit without packageQuantity, use a zero or negative packageQuantity, or enter a malformed barcode.",
      "result": "S1 AC4 tests only the case where both values are set. No AC defines pairing rules, positive-value rules, unit allowed values, or barcode format. Invalid product identity can be saved."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Create a one-step item with quantity 0",
      "method": "Use the S3 one-step form with a name and a quantity of 0, which is the new valid state for pantry rows.",
      "result": "S3 AC2 says only \"name and quantity.\" It does not say whether zero is accepted there, so the out-of-stock creation case is undefined."
    },
    {
